Hiring Team \
Prometheum

Dear Hiring Team,

I spent the last few years bartending while finishing a CS degree at Portland State, and the project I'm proudest of came directly out of that job: closing shift always ended with someone talking a rushed inventory count into their phone, and nobody ever turned it into anything useful. So I built the tool that does — a full-stack app in TypeScript, React, and Python that turns those voice notes into structured data in a relational database, with a parsing workflow I designed myself that cut wasted AI API calls by roughly 80 percent. It's the project I'd point to first, not because it's flashy, but because it's a real problem I understood firsthand, and I built it end to end — front end down to the database — using AI coding tools the way I'd want to on a team like yours: as a fast first draft I still have to read carefully and correct.

That's a lot of why the Software Engineer 1 posting caught my attention. Starting on small, well-scoped pieces of a TypeScript and React codebase and growing into more as I learn the system is exactly the kind of ramp-up I'm looking for, and I liked that the posting treats AI-assisted development as standard practice rather than something to hide.

My coursework — systems programming, data structures, digital circuits, SQL databases — gave me the fundamentals to actually reason about how a request moves from a front end through an API into a database, not just pattern-match. I'll say plainly that I don't have a finance or blockchain background. What I do have is practice picking up an unfamiliar domain quickly and getting the details right when something's going to be relied on by other people — that's most of what my community research and grant-reporting work before this actually was.

One more thing worth mentioning: I'm used to working under real time pressure. Bar management doesn't leave room for things to wait, and I don't expect that to translate one-to-one into production engineering, but staying calm, prioritizing, and following through on the unglamorous parts of a job has served me well everywhere I've had to do it.

I'd welcome the chance to bring that combination — a working full-stack project, real AI-assisted development habits, and a track record of learning fast under pressure — to your team. Happy to walk through the Bar Inventory App in more detail whenever that's useful.

Sincerely, \
Matt Loera
